---
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import TargetImage from "../../../../assets/courses/ap-csp-lua/img/bp-starfield-target.png";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";
import { Code as CodeSnippet } from "astro:components";

//#region CodeSnippets
const Fill = `for i = 1, 150 do
    starX[i] = math.random(0, 800)
    starY[i] = math.random(0, 600)
    starSpeed[i] = math.random(20, 220)
end`;

const Move = `for i = 1, #starX do
    starX[i] = starX[i] - starSpeed[i] * dt

    if starX[i] < 0 then
        starX[i] = 800
        starY[i] = math.random(0, 600)
    end
end`;

const Shade = `local shade = starSpeed[i] / 220
love.graphics.setColor(shade, shade, shade)`;
//#endregion
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a href="/courses/ap-csp-lua/" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3">
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Bonus Project: Starfield</h1>
        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <section id="objective">
          <h2>What This Is</h2>

          <p>
            <b class="text-sn-theme">The window becomes a ship going
            somewhere.</b> A hundred and fifty stars stream past, and the ones
            that pass fastest are the brightest, because they are the closest.
            Two lines of arithmetic buy you the illusion of depth on a flat
            screen.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b>This is extra credit and it is optional.</b> Nothing later in the
            course depends on it. It exists because you finished early, or
            because you want to build something you can show somebody.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <section id="before">
          <h2>Before You Start</h2>

          <p>
            <b class="text-sn-theme">You need everything through session 20.</b>{" "}
            Tables, <CodeSnippet code="#" lang="lua" inline />, and going
            through a table with a loop.{" "}
            <b>Nothing here is new</b> &mdash; this is the smallest project on
            the list that would be impossible without tables, which is exactly
            why it sits here.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b>Three tables, not one.</b> You need an x, a y and a speed for
            every star, and a table holds one list of values. So you keep three
            lists side by side and{" "}
            <b class="text-sn-theme">use the same index in all three</b>:{" "}
            <CodeSnippet code="starX[7]" lang="lua" inline />,{" "}
            <CodeSnippet code="starY[7]" lang="lua" inline /> and{" "}
            <CodeSnippet code="starSpeed[7]" lang="lua" inline /> are the three
            facts about star number seven.
          </p>

          <p class="text-body-secondary">
            Copy <CodeSnippet code="template-game" lang="txt" inline /> and
            rename it <CodeSnippet code="bp-starfield" lang="txt" inline />. No
            new files, no libraries, nothing to install.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <section id="tasks">
          <h2>Build It</h2>

          <p>
            Four stages. <b class="text-sn-theme">Run it after every one.</b> A
            stage that works is worth more than three stages you cannot test.
          </p>

          <h3 class="mt-4">1. Fill the sky</h3>

          <p>
            Three empty tables at the top of the file, then one loop that fills
            all three:
          </p>

          <Code code={Fill} lang="lua" frame="code" showLineNumbers={false} />

          <p>
            <b>That loop runs once, at the top of the file</b>, not in{" "}
            <CodeSnippet code="love.update" lang="lua" inline />. Put it in the
            update by mistake and you get a hundred and fifty brand-new stars
            sixty times a second, which looks like static.
          </p>

          <p class="text-body-secondary">
            Draw them with a second loop in{" "}
            <CodeSnippet code="love.draw" lang="lua" inline /> &mdash; a small
            white rectangle at each{" "}
            <CodeSnippet code="starX[i], starY[i]" lang="lua" inline />. Nothing
            moves yet, and it should already look like a sky.
          </p>

          <h3 class="mt-4">2. Make them move</h3>

          <p>
            One loop in <CodeSnippet code="love.update" lang="lua" inline />,
            taking each star's own speed off its own x:
          </p>

          <Code code={Move} lang="lua" frame="code" showLineNumbers={false} />

          <p>
            <b class="text-sn-theme">The wrap-around is what makes it
            endless.</b> A star that leaves the left edge is not deleted &mdash;
            it is moved back to the right at a new height, and it becomes a
            different star. A hundred and fifty stars, reused forever.
          </p>

          <h3 class="mt-4">3. Depth</h3>

          <p>
            <b>This is the whole project.</b> A star's speed already says how
            close it is, so use the same number for its brightness:
          </p>

          <Code code={Shade} lang="lua" frame="code" showLineNumbers={false} />

          <p>
            Dividing by the top speed turns a number between 20 and 220 into a
            number between roughly 0.1 and 1, which is the scale{" "}
            <CodeSnippet code="setColor" lang="lua" inline /> wants. Slow stars
            come out dim, fast stars come out white.
          </p>

          <div class="alert alert-info">
            <b>Try it without this step and then with it.</b> The motion is
            identical either way. Everything that makes it look like distance
            comes from the brightness, and that is worth seeing back to back.
          </div>

          <h3 class="mt-4">4. Size, for free</h3>

          <p>
            Use the same <CodeSnippet code="shade" lang="lua" inline /> to make
            the near stars slightly wider than the far ones. One number, three
            jobs.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <section id="sample-output">
          <h2>What Success Looks Like</h2>

          <div class="text-center my-4">
            <Image
              src={TargetImage}
              alt="A black 800 by 600 window filled with small white and gray dots of several brightnesses, scattered like a star field"
              width={800}
              class="img-fluid rounded"
            />
          </div>

          <p>
            A still picture cannot show the motion, so look at the{" "}
            <b class="text-sn-theme">brightness instead</b>. The bright wide
            dots are the fast ones and they are the ones that will cross the
            screen while you watch. The faint ones barely crawl.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <section id="extend">
          <h2>Then Make It Yours</h2>

          <ul>
            <li class="mb-2">
              <b>Turn.</b> Steer the whole field with Left and Right by adding a
              little to every star's y as well as taking off its x. The near
              stars should swing further than the far ones, and if you use{" "}
              <CodeSnippet code="shade" lang="lua" inline /> again you get that
              for nothing.
            </li>
            <li class="mb-2">
              <b>Go to warp.</b> Hold a key and multiply every speed. Then draw
              each star as a short line from where it is to where it was,
              instead of a dot.{" "}
              <b class="text-sn-theme">That is a fourth table</b> and it is
              worth the trouble.
            </li>
            <li class="mb-2">
              <b>Color the stars.</b> Real ones are not white. Three more
              tables, or one number per star that you turn into a color.
            </li>
            <li class="mb-2">
              <b>Fly through it.</b> Put a ship in the middle that the field
              moves around. Now it is a game with no enemies, which is one
              enemy short of a game.
            </li>
            <li class="mb-2">
              <b>Count them.</b> Change 150 to 5000 and find where your computer
              starts to struggle. Then work out what is actually expensive:
              the arithmetic, or the drawing.
            </li>
          </ul>

          <p class="text-body-secondary">
            <b>Submit whatever you have.</b> A field of stars that moves still
            earns credit, and so does a finished one with a bug you can
            describe.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <h1
          class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash"
        >
          Project Complete
        </h1>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
